.Dd September 24, 2026
.Dt RECOVERY 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm recovery
.Nd recovery system and repair procedures
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The recovery system runs from a RAM root.
It boots from the install stick's first partition or the disk's recovery
entry.
To select the latter from the running system, use
.Ql bootctl trial recovery
and reboot.
.Pp
The recovery kernel permits root to write
.Pa /dev/diskN
and uses the read-write driver policy.
The recovery system mounts nothing from the disk it repairs, except that
its init reads the recovery key file described below with the recovery
partition mounted read-only, and unmounts it before anything else starts.
.Ss Startup
The recovery system runs
.Pa /sbin/init
built as
.Ql init-recovery .
It starts a shell on the console without a login: the image has no
accounts of the machine's to check against.
It starts the SSH server only where root has an authorized key: one built
into the image, or, on a recovery system booted from an installed disk's
own recovery entry, whose boot line has
.Ql rach2.slot=recovery ,
the keys in the file
.Pa boot/recovery_authorized_keys
that
.Xr rach2-install 8
put on that disk's recovery partition.
Init finds the disk by the identity
.Ql rach2.boot=MBRID=ID,1
names and the partition by its label,
.Ql RACH2RECOV ,
and adds the file's keys to root's.
It refuses a file larger than 16384 bytes, one that is not text, one that
holds a private key, and one with no line that is a key.
The install stick's recovery system reads no such file.
Init reports on the console what it found:
.Dl init: recovery key: taken from /dev/disk0p2
.Dl init: a key was built into this recovery image, so SSH is on
.Dl init: no key was built into this recovery image, and its recovery partition's key turns SSH on
.Dl init: no key was built into this recovery image, so SSH is off
A restore of the whole disk from the stick's image leaves no recovery key
file, since the image has none.
With
.Ql rach2.fsck
on the boot line, init first runs
.Ql e2fsck -p
on each of
.Pa /dev/disk0p1
through
.Pa /dev/disk0p4
whose superblock has the ext2 magic, and reports
.Dl init: e2fsck: /dev/disk0pN: status S
Status 0 means clean, 1 repaired, 4 faults left and 8 an error.
Without the token a recovery boot repairs nothing.
The network is configured by the kernel from the
.Ql rach2.ip=
boot setting, or by DHCP when that setting is
.Ql none .
.Pp
The console shell is root's, so physical access to a machine is control of
it.
Anyone at its keyboard who boots the install stick, or chooses the disk's
recovery entry from the boot menu, has a root shell that can write the
machine's disks.
The boot menus ask for no password, so a boot line can also be edited to
add
.Ql rach2.rescue ,
which starts the kernel debug shell described below; that shell
authenticates nobody.
.Ss Programs
The recovery image includes
.Nm rach2-install ,
.Nm rach2-recover ,
.Nm reflash ,
.Nm bootctl ,
.Nm growroot ,
.Nm growset ,
.Nm growboot ,
.Nm diskctl ,
.Nm e2fsck ,
.Nm resize2fs ,
.Nm update ,
.Nm rescue-static ,
.Nm ping ,
.Nm dmesg
and the BusyBox tools.
.Ss Update procedures
The procedure depends on what is being replaced:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Kernel
From the running system, run
.Ql bootctl kernel FILE ,
reboot, read
.Ql bootctl status
and run
.Ql bootctl commit .
.It Root file system
From recovery, run
.Ql rach2-install --update IMAGE /dev/diskN .
Mount the install stick's exFAT partition and use its
.Pa rach2-metal-disk.ext2
as
.Ar IMAGE ;
the RAM root cannot store this 1 GiB file.
This preserves the accounts, SSH policy and keys.
The recovery archive stores account files and SSH policy under
.Pa rach2-config-a/etc ,
host keys under
.Pa rach2-config-a/etc/dropbear ,
and each account's home
.Pa .ssh
contents under
.Pa rach2-config-a/keys/UID .
Root's keys use UID 0.
The home comes from passwd, and only the first entry for a shared UID
supplies keys.
Numeric directory names cannot collide by case on FAT32.
Within each account's .ssh, names that differ only in case are refused
before the root is written, with both paths in the diagnostic.
Normal updates preserve .ssh in every passwd home, including homes outside
.Pa /home ,
with their owners and modes.
Before restoring retained keys, the image's .ssh is removed from every
account home outside /home, including accounts with no archived keys.
A resumed update, or one whose old root cannot be read, restores these keys
from the archive, with modes 700 for directories and 600 for files.
Host keys are owned by 0:0; account keys return to the home from the
archived passwd with its uid and gid.
Missing homes are created with mode 700 and that owner.
Keys for UIDs absent from that file are ignored.
An older archive without UID key directories cannot restore account keys.
The archive has two slots,
.Pa rach2-config-a
and
.Pa rach2-config-b .
A slot is complete only when it contains all three account files and its
.Pa generation
file contains a decimal integer from 1 to 2147483647.
A slot with a missing or invalid generation is ignored.
Complete legacy archives count as generation 0; any generation file in
one is ignored.
Readers choose the highest generation, breaking ties in this order:
.Pa rach2-config ,
.Pa rach2-config.new ,
.Pa rach2-config.old ,
.Pa rach2-config-a ,
then
.Pa rach2-config-b .
The writer rebuilds B when A was chosen, and A otherwise.
It removes the destination's generation file, syncs, removes the destination,
writes its contents, syncs, writes the next generation, and syncs again.
Only then does it remove all three legacy names, which it never writes.
An install leaves A at generation 1.
Before writing an updated root, the installer writes B with the update mark,
first writing A with the mark if needed.
After restoring the accounts it writes A without the mark, so A is the
resting slot after a complete run.
A counter at 2147483647 stops further archive writes.
Recovery remains the saved default until the trial root has been verified;
then run
.Ql bootctl commit .
.It Recovery partition
Use
.Xr reflash 8
from the disk's recovery entry.
The partition being written is not mounted.
The saved default remains the candidate for this write.
A torn write is repaired from the install stick.
.El
.Pp
The machine's disk stays in its bay.
These procedures run over SSH or at the console.
Do not make a write that cannot be undone from the stick.
The install stick has room beside its two images for saved copies of
the 64 MiB boot and recovery partitions.
This reserve does not cover a backup of a grown root.
Check the space required by the actual save set before writing.
.Ss Recovery menu
.Xr rach2-recover 8
offers these choices:
.Bl -enum
.It
List the machine's disks, with model, serial, size in bytes, partition table
and partitions.
.It
Check a file system.
.It
Put a saved image back on a disk.
.It
Install a kernel on a boot partition.
.It
Start a shell.
.It
Leave.
.El
.Pp
Each step can be left without taking action and reports its result in one
line.
.Ss Kernel debug shell
The boot option
.Ql rach2.rescue
skips init and enters the kernel debug shell.
Its
.Ql exec PROGRAM
command runs a program from the root.
The console belongs to the shell.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /root/.ssh/authorized_keys
.It Pa /etc/rach2/sshd.conf
SSH policy, including the
.Ql root-password-login
setting.
.It Pa /root/.ssh/authorized_keys
Root's SSH authorized keys.
.It Pa /proc/disk
Disk records.
.It Pa /proc/cmdline
The boot command line.
.It Pa /dev/bootlog
The boot log.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr disk 7 ,
.Xr bootctl 8 ,
.Xr growroot 8 ,
.Xr rach2-install 8 ,
.Xr rach2-recover 8 ,
.Xr reflash 8
